# MongoDB

## Indexes

- Every query pattern needs a supporting index.
- Compound indexes: most selective field first.
- Add indexes on the schema, not at runtime.
- Review with `explain()` for slow queries.

## Query performance

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD — projection, lean, limit
Beat.find({ genre }).select("title coverUrl bpm").lean().limit(20);

// ❌ BAD — fetches everything
Beat.find({ genre });
```

- Always use `.lean()` for read-only queries.
- Always use `.select()` to project only needed fields.
- Always use `.limit()` — never unbounded queries.

## N+1 prevention

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — N+1 queries in a loop
for (const beat of beats) {
  const producer = await User.findById(beat.producerId);
}

// ✅ GOOD — batch query
const producerIds = [...new Set(beats.map(b => b.producerId.toString()))];
const producers = await User.find({ _id: { $in: producerIds } }).lean();
const producerMap = new Map(producers.map(p => [p._id.toString(), p]));
```

## Aggregation

- Use aggregation pipelines for complex queries (stats, grouping, lookups).
- `$match` early to reduce pipeline volume.
- Use `$project` to limit fields in pipeline stages.

## Schema design

- **Embed** when data is always read together and bounded in size.
- **Reference** when data is shared across documents or unbounded.
- Review read/write patterns before choosing.
- Keep document size under 1MB (well under for performance).
- Plan for schema evolution — use optional fields, avoid breaking changes.

## Transactions

- Use `withTransaction()` from `@/lib/db` for multi-document writes.
- Transactions require replica set (or `ALLOW_NON_TRANSACTIONAL_FALLBACK` in dev).
- Keep transactions short — no external API calls inside.
